  completeExplanation: 'Curiosity Chapter 8 begins with a timeless verse from the ancient Tamil classic Thirukkural: "If it does not rain well, even the mighty ocean will be drained." Aavi and Thirav enjoy cold shikanji (lemonade) on a summer afternoon, debating whether hard ice and fluid water are different substances. Activity 8.1 shows ice melting into liquid water, proving they are different states of the same substance. In Activity 8.2, a tablespoon of water on an impermeable steel plate disappears into the atmosphere as invisible water vapour. Evaporation is the conversion of water into its vapour state and occurs continuously at all temperatures. In Activity 8.3, ice placed in a glass tumbler produces droplets on the dry outer surface. Activity 8.4 measures a steady increase in total mass on a digital balance, conclusively proving that ambient water vapour condenses upon contact with the cold surface rather than seeping through the glass. The amount of water vapour in air is termed humidity. Water exists in three physical states: solid (ice, fixed shape), liquid (water, flows, fixed volume), and gas (water vapour, expands freely). Phase transitions occur by adding or removing heat: solid melts to liquid, liquid evaporates to gas, gas condenses to liquid, and liquid freezes to solid. The chapter highlights Atmospheric Water Generators (AWG) that harvest drinkable water from humid air. In Activity 8.7 and 8.8, evaporation rate is shown to increase with larger exposed surface area (wide plate vs narrow cap), higher temperature (sunlight vs shade), higher wind speed, and lower humidity. Evaporative cooling occurs because evaporating molecules absorb latent heat from their surroundings. This principle underlies traditional clay matkas, surahis, and pot-in-pot coolers (Zeer pots with wet sand), keeping food and water cold without electricity. In Activity 8.10, warm rising water vapour condenses around smoke/dust nuclei to form clouds inside a bottle. When cloud droplets coalesce, precipitation occurs as rain, snow, or hail. Activity 8.11 maps the global water cycle, underscoring that despite abundant oceans, accessible freshwater is scarce, necessitating conservation, rainwater harvesting, and traditional stepwell/kund revival: "Water is our responsibility before it is our right."',
